Undifferentiated Peripheral Neuropathy
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Distal symmetric tingling/numbness (stocking-glove pattern) | Complete absence of diabetes mellitus or B12 deficiency | Idiopathic or non-diagnostic findings on EMG/NCS

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-039
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal exacerbation of severe foot burning and unsteadiness, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute spinal cord compression or acute arterial occlusion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset, severe neuropathic pain and gait instability. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out cauda equina syndrome, acute demyelinating polyneuropathy, or critical limb ischemia, preventing irreversible nerve or tissue damage prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My feet feel like they are constantly on fire, I cannot feel the floor properly, and I feel like I am walking on cotton wool.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports symmetrical numbness and tingling in the distal extremities, which has had a gradual onset progressing in a stocking-glove distribution over months, but acutely flared tonight causing severe sleep disruption. The patient describes a severe burning or prickling neuropathic pain. There is a subjective feeling of walking on cotton, causing unsteadiness in the dark. The patient denies significant motor weakness or overt autonomic dysfunction (no profound orthostasis or bowel/bladder incontinence).
Functional Status: Mobilizing very slowly; independent but high fall risk in low light.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired proprioception leading to immediate fall risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 78 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued from sleep disruption and rubbing their feet constantly. Systemic review incidentally notes a recent >5% weight loss, anorexia, and a normal baseline metabolic panel; these symptoms are being investigated separately but compound the patient's general frailty.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination of the lower extremities reveals a mild loss of vibratory and pinprick sensation symmetrically in the toes up to the mid-foot. There are decreased Achilles tendon reflexes bilaterally, while patellar reflexes remain intact. There is an absolute absence of significant motor weakness (5/5 strength in all muscle groups) or profound muscle atrophy. Cardiovascular exam confirms strong, bounding dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: Dim lighting in the hallways; loose rugs present throughout the house.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes no neuropathic analgesics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Peripheral Neuropathy - Status: Acute symptomatic exacerbation of chronic sensory nerve damage.
Differentials:

Acute Spinal Cord Compression - Ruled out definitively due to intact bowel/bladder sphincter tone, absence of a sharp sensory level on the trunk, and preserved motor strength.

Critical Limb Ischemia - Ruled out completely due to the presence of bounding distal pulses, warm extremities, and lack of ischemic pallor or rubor.

Guillain-Barré Syndrome - Ruled out due to the chronic, gradual onset, strict sensory nature of the complaint, and completely preserved motor power.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Fall risk).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s severe sensory pain and new gait instability required an immediate after-hours assessment to exclude a catastrophic compressive or ischemic neurological event. Telephone triage accurately identified the critical necessity to rule out cauda equina syndrome or an acute arterial occlusion. Detailed neurological and vascular examination definitively confirmed a distal, symmetrical, purely sensory neuropathy with perfect peripheral perfusion. By identifying this as a chronic neuropathic pain flare rather than an acute structural emergency, the clinician safely managed the patient’s distress and fall risk at home, completely avoiding an unnecessary, high-priority emergency department transfer for emergency imaging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated a trial of low-dose neuropathic pain modulators (e.g., pregabalin/gabapentin or low-dose TCA) for symptomatic nighttime relief. The expected investigation results include nerve conduction studies revealing mild axonal sensorimotor polyneuropathy without a clear metabolic or toxic etiology, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via specialist neurology review.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent falls caused by poor foot sensation, implement a strict "lights on" policy for any nighttime mobilization and remove all loose rugs from your walking path; ensuring maximum visual input will compensate for the lack of sensory feedback from your feet.
Home-Based Services: Referral generated for a community podiatrist and occupational therapist for a home safety assessment.
DME/Safety: Advised strict use of supportive, hard-soled footwear even while indoors.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for a comprehensive metabolic and vitamin screen (B12, Folate, HbA1c, SPEP) and formal neurology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops sudden, profound weakness in the legs, loses control of their bowel or bladder, or experiences numbness spreading rapidly up the legs to the groin.

